    {
      "abstained": false,
      "answer": "Hand hygiene can be performed by using either plain soap or products including antiseptic agents, which have the property of inactivating microorganisms or inhibiting their growth with different action spectra. Examples include alcohols, chlorhexidine gluconate, chlorine derivatives, iodine, chloroxylenol, quaternary ammonium compounds, and triclosan. Hexachlorophene and iodophors are also listed among the antiseptics, and alcohols are used at a typical concentration of 60-80 % with fast speed of action and no residual activity.",
      "citations": [
        "who-hand-hygiene-guidelines-summary-2009#part-iii-guideline-implementation-p-39-0",
        "who-hand-hygiene-guidelines-summary-2009#fungi-spores-p-39-0"
