    {
      "abstained": false,
      "answer": "Yes, freezing can harm vaccines: inactivated vaccines, even when exposed to freezing temperatures, may not appear frozen, giving no visual indication of reduced or lost potency. Vaccines stored at inappropriate temperatures should not be administered unless public health authorities or the manufacturer confirm they are safe and effective based on stability data. To reduce the risk of freezing vaccines during transport, do not use frozen gel packs or coolant packs from original vaccine shipments to pack refrigerated vaccines, because they can still freeze vaccines even if they are conditioned or appear to be \"sweating\".",
      "citations": [
